      "Runs on gasoline or propane",
      "CO Shield automatic shutoff",
      "Spec sheet lists under 3% THD, safe for electronics",
      "About 39 lb",
      "Rated 53 dBA at 23 feet"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1,850 running watts on gas (1,665 on propane) covers essentials only",
      "Costs noticeably more per watt than an open-frame"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to get clean inverter power, propane flexibility and a CO shutoff in one light box.",
    "description": "If you need quiet, clean power more than you need raw watts, the 201183 is the budget inverter to buy. Champion rates it at 2,500 starting and 1,850 running watts on gasoline and 1,665 running watts on propane, from a 79cc engine. It weighs about 39 pounds, Champion lists 53 dBA at 23 feet, and the spec sheet gives under 3% total harmonic distortion, so laptops, TVs and furnace control boards are fine on it. CO Shield shuts the engine off if carbon monoxide builds up. Champion rates it for up to 11.5 hours on gasoline and up to 34 hours on a 20-lb propane tank, and propane is the bigger deal for a budget buyer: it stores for years without going stale. The catch is the cost per running watt, which is roughly two to three times what an open-frame costs. It will run a refrigerator, lights, a router and phone chargers. It will not start a well pump or central air, and nothing about it changes that."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-igen2550c",
    "name": "Westinghouse iGen2550c",
    "category": "Inverter, 1,900W Running / 2,550W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Cheap Inverter for RVs",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 1800,
    "pros": [
      "CO sensor with automatic shutdown",
      "TT-30R RV outlet on a 2,550W inverter",
      "Westinghouse lists as low as 52 dBA",
      "About 42-46 lb",
      "Up to 11-12 hours at 25% load, per Westinghouse"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Gasoline only",
      "Small tank of about 1.1 gallons",
      "Some listings give 1,800 running watts, others 1,900; check the label"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A light, quiet budget inverter with a CO sensor and an RV plug. A good second generator and a fine first one for an apartment-sized load.",
