      "Parallel-capable with a WEN kit"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No CO sensor on this model (the 56235iX adds one)",
      "1-gallon tank",
      "2-year warranty is shorter than Champion or Westinghouse"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A long-running, cheap inverter that is fine for camping and short outages. Buy the iX version if it will run anywhere near a house.",
    "description": "The 56235i has been one of the cheapest name-brand inverter generators for years, and it is still a sensible buy if you understand what is missing. WEN rates it at 1,900 running and 2,350 surge watts, it weighs about 39 pounds, and WEN lists 51 dB at quarter load. The fuel shutoff valve sounds minor but matters for a budget generator that sits unused. Running the carburetor dry before storage prevents the gummed jets that stop most neglected small engines. It can be paired with a second WEN inverter using a parallel kit. What it gives up is the CO sensor. WEN sells a 56235iX version with a CO shutdown sensor, and for anything running near a house, the iX is worth the extra money. The 1-gallon tank means refueling during an all-night outage, and the 2-year warranty is a year shorter than Champion's or Westinghouse's. For camping, tailgating and short outages, it does the job well."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Cheap Generators",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-cheap-generator/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the cheapest generator worth buying?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For home backup, the cheapest generator we would buy is a name-brand open-frame around 3,500 running watts with a CO shutoff, like the Westinghouse WGen3600cv. It has enough power for a refrigerator, sump pump and lights, it shuts itself off when carbon monoxide builds up, and parts and a warranty stand behind it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cheap inverter generators any good?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Name-brand budget inverters like the Champion 201183, Westinghouse iGen2550c and WEN 56235i are good for what they are: quiet, light and clean enough for electronics. They cost more per running watt than open-frames, and around 1,800-1,900 running watts limits them to essential loads. No-name inverters are a gamble on parts and support."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many watts do I need from a cheap generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Add up the running watts of everything you want to run at once, then add the starting surge of the largest motor load. A refrigerator, sump pump, lights and a router typically land between 2,500 and 4,000 running watts with surge. That is open-frame territory for most budget buyers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a cheap generator safe to run in a garage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. No generator is safe to run in a garage, even with the door open, and a CO shutoff does not change that. Run it outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors and windows, and keep a battery CO alarm inside."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do cheap generators damage electronics?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not usually, but budget open-frames often have higher harmonic distortion than inverters, and some electronics such as furnace boards, variable-speed appliances and some UPS units can react badly. Inverters like the Champion 201183 list under 3% THD. For sensitive gear, use an inverter, a power station or a UPS."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is dual fuel worth it on a cheap generator?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually yes if the generator will be stored more than it is used. Propane does not go stale, so a dual-fuel unit is far more likely to start after a year in the garage. You give up a little power on propane; the DuroMax XP5500HX drops from 4,500 to 4,275 running watts, for example."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a cheap generator power my whole house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not in the sense most people mean. A budget generator can run essential circuits through a transfer switch or interlock, but it cannot run central air, an electric range and a water heater together. For that you need a much larger portable or a standby unit; see our whole-house generator guides."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Emergency Preparedness",
    tags: [
      "cheap generators",
      "cheap generator",
      "inexpensive generator",
      "inexpensive generators",
      "budget generator"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated November 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Cheap Generators</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Five budget generators that are actually worth the money, compared on cost per running watt, CO shutoff, fuel and the corners each one cuts.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#westinghouse-wgen3600cv"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            See the Price Tiers
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="12.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#tiers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Price Tiers</a>
          <a href="#cost-per-watt" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Cost per Watt</a>
          <a href="#corners" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Corners Cut</a>
          <a href="#co-shutoff" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Cheapest With CO Shutoff</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Our Picks</a>
          <a href="#open-frame" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. When Open-Frame Is Fine</a>
          <a href="#false-economy" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. False Economy</a>
          <a href="#thd" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. THD and Electronics</a>
          <a href="#hidden-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Hidden Costs</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A cheap generator is a good buy when it has enough running watts for your real loads and a CO-sensing shutoff. Below about $500 you can get both. What you give up is quiet operation, electric start, clean power or 240V, and the trick is choosing which of those you can live without.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="tiers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Cheap Actually Buys at Each Price Tier
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cheap generators are not one category. The money you spend decides how many watts you get and which safety features come with them, and the jumps between tiers are steep. Prices move constantly with sales and hurricane season, so we talk in tiers instead of quoting a price that will be wrong next week. Roughly, the tiers fall around $300, around $500 and around $800, and a careful buyer can get something useful in each.
          </p>

          <p>
            At the bottom, typically under about $300 on sale, you are buying either a 3,000-4,000 watt open-frame unit from a value brand or a small no-name inverter. The open-frames in this tier are genuinely capable. The Westinghouse WGen3600cv has dipped into this range during sales, and it pairs 3,600 running watts with a CO sensor and a fuel gauge. The no-name inverters that sell here are the risky part of the tier: thin documentation, no parts support and often no CO shutoff.
          </p>

          <p>
            Around $500 is where budget inverters with CO shutoff live, and where open-frames gain electric start, dual fuel and, occasionally, a 240V twist-lock outlet. The Champion 201183 and Westinghouse iGen2550c are the inverters to look at. The DuroMax XP5500HX is the open-frame that adds the most for the money. This tier is the sweet spot for most households.
          </p>

          <p>
            Around $800 you are no longer shopping cheap. You are shopping mid-range. That money buys a 4,000-5,000 watt inverter with a CO sensor or a 7,000-9,500 watt open-frame with a 50-amp outlet. If your loads genuinely need that, look at the <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> or the <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">5,000 watt class</a> instead of stretching a budget unit past its limits.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running Watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You Usually Give Up</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Around $300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Open-frame, value brand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-3,600W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Electric start, 240V, quiet operation</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Around $500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small inverter or dual fuel open-frame</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,800-4,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either watts (inverter) or quiet (open-frame)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Around $800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid-size inverter or large open-frame</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,700-9,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Nothing critical; this is no longer cheap</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-per-watt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cost per Running Watt: The Number to Compare
          </h2>

          <p>
            The fairest way to compare cheap generators is to divide the price by running watts, not peak watts. Peak watts last a second or two while a motor starts. Running watts are what the generator sustains, and that is what decides whether your refrigerator and sump pump can run together for eight hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do the arithmetic and the gap between generator types is large. An open-frame with 3,600 running watts that sells for around $400 costs roughly 11 cents per running watt. A 1,850-running-watt inverter at around $600 costs over 30 cents per running watt. The inverter is not overpriced. You are paying for quiet, clean power and low weight rather than raw capacity. But if raw capacity is what you need, an open-frame wins on value by a factor of two or three.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost per watt also exposes the classic budget mistake, which is buying a small inverter for home backup because it was the cheapest thing on the shelf. A 2,000-watt inverter will keep a refrigerator, lights and a router going. It will not start most well pumps, a 1/2 hp sump pump plus a fridge at the same moment, or any central air system. If you end up buying a second generator because the first one was too small, the cheap one was not cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before comparing prices, write down what you actually need to run at the same time and add the largest starting surge. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical running and starting figures for common appliances, and <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/">what size generator do I need</a> walks through the math. Then compare only generators that clear that number with about 20% to spare, and pick the lowest cost per running watt among them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare price per running watt, then check for a CO shutoff.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A generator that is 20% cheaper but cannot start your sump pump is a waste of money. One without a CO shutoff costs less because it skips the feature most likely to save a life.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="corners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Corners Cheap Generators Cut
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every generator under about $500 is missing something, and the useful question is whether the missing piece matters to you. The corners manufacturers cut are predictable, and some are much more serious than others.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">CO-sensing shutoff</h3>

          <p>
            This is the one that matters most. Carbon monoxide poisoning from portable generators kills people every year, usually because the generator was run in a garage, under a carport or near an open window. A CO-sensing shutoff kills the engine when CO builds up around the unit. Westinghouse calls it CO Sensor, Champion calls it CO Shield, DuroMax calls it CO Alert and WEN calls it Watchdog. Many of the cheapest units, including older or base versions of name-brand models, leave it out. On a generator that will run near a house, we would not save money here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fuel gauge</h3>

          <p>
            A missing fuel gauge sounds trivial until you are guessing at 2 a.m. whether you have enough gasoline to get to morning. Cheap open-frames often have nothing, or a cap with a tiny float. A real gauge, like the one on the WGen3600cv, lets you plan refueling around sleep instead of around a stalled engine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Power quality (THD)</h3>

          <p>
            Total harmonic distortion measures how far the output wave strays from a clean sine wave. Inverters routinely list under 3%. Budget open-frames often do not publish a figure at all, which usually means it is high. More on what that means in practice below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Parts, support and warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Champion and Westinghouse list 3-year limited warranties on models like the 201183 and WGen3600cv, and WEN lists 2 years on the 56235i. More important than the number is whether you can actually get a carburetor, a recoil assembly or a voltage regulator four years from now. The name brands sell parts and publish manuals. Many no-name inverters on marketplaces vanish within a season, and a generator that cannot be repaired is disposable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-shutoff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Cheapest Generators With CO Shutoff
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you take one thing from this page, buy a generator with a CO-sensing shutoff. The good news is that you no longer have to pay much for it. Every major value brand now sells budget models with one, and the price premium over the no-sensor version of the same machine is usually modest.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the open-frame class, the Westinghouse WGen3600cv is the cheapest CO-sensing generator we would trust with home backup. The 'c' in Westinghouse model numbers means CO sensor, so a WGen3600v is the same generator without it. The DuroMax XP5500HX adds a CO alert shutoff to a dual-fuel, electric-start unit. In the inverter class, the Champion 201183 and Westinghouse iGen2550c both shut down on CO, and WEN's 56235iX is the CO-sensing version of the 56235i we list here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read model numbers carefully, because the difference is often a single letter. Retailers sell both versions side by side, and the cheaper listing is frequently the one without the sensor. Check that the listing says CO shutoff, CO Shield, CO Sensor or CO Alert, not just 'CO safe' or 'outdoor use only.'
          </p>

          <p>
            A CO shutoff does not make a generator safe to run indoors, in a garage with the door open, or under an eave. It is a backstop for mistakes, not permission to make them. Placement is still the first line of defense, and <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers distance, wind direction and rain. Pair any generator with a battery CO alarm inside the house, whatever the generator's own sensor says.
          </p>

