        <div id="open-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When a Cheap Open-Frame Is Fine
          </h2>

          <p>
            Open-frame generators get dismissed as loud and crude, but for many households a cheap open-frame is the right answer. If your goal is keeping a refrigerator, a freezer, a sump pump and some lights running during an outage, an open-frame gives you the most watts for the money by a wide margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Home backup is the clearest case. The generator sits in the yard 20 feet from the house, nobody is sleeping next to it, and noise is a minor annoyance next to a flooded basement. Refrigerators, freezers, sump pumps, well pumps and space heaters with simple controls run happily on open-frame power. Motor loads in particular need the surge capacity open-frames provide cheaply.
          </p>

          <p>
            Job sites and workshops are the other good fit. Circular saws, compressors and corded drills are built for rough power, and the extra watts let you start a compressor without stalling. Weight matters less when the generator rides in a truck bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            A cheap open-frame is also the right choice if you are buying your first generator for an area with occasional outages and you are not sure how much you will use it. Spending less on a capable open-frame, then adding a <a href="/best-ups-battery-backup/">UPS battery backup</a> for the computer and router, often beats spending twice as much on an inverter.
          </p>

          <p>
            What an open-frame does not like is being judged by campground standards. If you need to run it at a campsite, beside a camper, or in a tight neighborhood where noise rules apply, look at an inverter instead. Our <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/">camping generator</a> and <a href="/best-quiet-generator/">quiet generator</a> guides cover those cases.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="false-economy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Cheap Is False Economy
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are a few situations where buying the cheapest generator costs more in the end. Knowing them up front saves the second purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first is undersizing. If the generator cannot start your largest motor load, you will end up doing load rotation in the dark, switching circuits by hand every hour, or buying a second unit. A well pump or central air system needs a generator sized for its starting surge, and that rules out every small inverter on this page. <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">What size generator runs a well pump</a> gives the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second is skipping 240V when you need it. Most cheap generators have only 120V outlets. If your house runs on a well, or you want to connect through a transfer switch to power a whole panel, you need a 120/240V outlet such as an L14-30. The DuroMax XP5500HX is the budget exception here. Our <a href="/best-240v-generator/">240V generator guide</a> covers the rest.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is buying a no-name inverter for long-term backup. These units can be fine new, but when the recoil spring or the inverter board fails in year three, there is often no manual, no parts and no support line. A name-brand unit at a slightly higher price is the cheaper long-term buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fourth is fuel. A gasoline-only generator that sits for a year will often refuse to start because the gasoline has gone stale and gummed the carburetor. If you will mostly store the generator and use it a few times a year, a dual-fuel model that can run on propane is often the smarter budget buy, because propane does not go bad. Our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">dual fuel generator</a> and <a href="/best-propane-generator/">propane generator</a> guides go deeper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thd" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. THD and What Cheap Power Does to Electronics
          </h2>

          <p>
            Total harmonic distortion is the cheap-generator spec people ask about most and understand least. It describes how much the output waveform deviates from a pure sine wave. Utility power is typically low distortion, and inverter generators like the Champion 201183 list under 3%.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conventional open-frame generators produce power directly from an alternator spinning at a fixed speed, and their waveform depends on alternator design and load. Better open-frames hold distortion reasonably low. Budget ones often do not publish a figure. That is not automatically a problem, but it means you should not assume clean power.
          </p>

          <p>
            What actually suffers from high distortion? Resistive loads like space heaters, incandescent bulbs and coffee makers do not care. Simple induction motors in refrigerators and pumps mostly do not care either, though they can run warmer. The loads that do care are electronics with switching power supplies or sensitive control boards: some furnace igniter boards, variable-speed appliances, some battery chargers and some UPS units that reject generator power entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheap fix is not to buy a more expensive generator for everything. Run the heavy, tolerant loads on the open-frame and put the computer, router and TV on a small inverter, a <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">portable power station</a> or a UPS that the generator charges. If your furnace is picky, test it on the generator before a storm, not during one. Our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator comparison</a> explains the difference in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hidden-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. The Hidden Costs of a Cheap Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            The price tag is only part of what a generator costs. A budget buyer should plan for the rest before checkout, because skipping these items is how cheap generators end up unused or unsafe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cords come first. Running a refrigerator 25 feet from the house on a thin household extension cord causes voltage drop and overheated plugs. You need a heavy-gauge outdoor cord rated for the load, and our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size</a> guide gives the gauges. For more than a couple of appliances, a transfer switch or interlock with an inlet box is the safe way to connect, and it costs real money to install. See <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types and cost</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is next. Every generator needs oil, and new engines need their first oil change early. Fuel stabilizer for gasoline, a spare spark plug and a cover add up. <a href="/how-often-to-change-generator-oil/">How often to change generator oil</a> and <a href="/how-to-store-a-portable-generator/">how to store a portable generator</a> cover the routine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel is the running cost. A 4-gallon open-frame running at half load for 24 hours burns a lot of gasoline, and in a regional outage gas stations without power cannot pump. Plan to store fuel safely and rotate it. Dual-fuel models let you keep a few propane tanks on hand instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add it up and a budget generator often needs another $100-$300 in accessories before it is ready for a real outage, more if you install a transfer switch. That is not a reason to skip the cheap generator. It is a reason to keep part of the budget for the cord, the oil and a CO alarm rather than spending every dollar on watts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing peak watts instead of running watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Budget listings put the biggest number on the box, and that is almost always peak watts. Peak lasts a second or two while a motor starts. Running watts are what the generator holds for hours. Compare price against running watts, and size against the loads you need to run at the same time plus the largest starting surge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the version without the CO sensor to save a little</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many budget generators are sold in two versions that differ by one letter in the model number, and the cheaper listing usually lacks CO shutoff. The savings are small and the risk is not. Check the listing for CO Sensor, CO Shield, CO Alert or Watchdog before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a small inverter to run a well pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2,000-watt inverter is great for a fridge and lights, but most well pumps run on 240V and have a starting surge beyond what these units can deliver. If you are on a well, you need a 120/240V outlet and enough surge capacity, which means a larger open-frame or inverter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a gasoline generator full for a year</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gasoline goes stale in months, gums the carburetor, and is the most common reason a budget generator will not start when the power goes out. Add stabilizer, run the carburetor dry with the fuel shutoff if your unit has one, or buy dual fuel and store propane instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spending the whole budget on the generator</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator without a heavy-gauge cord, oil, a CO alarm in the house and a safe way to connect is not ready for an outage. Keep $100 to $300 of the budget for accessories, and more if you plan to install a transfer switch or interlock kit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

