          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the cheapest generator worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For home backup, the cheapest generator we would buy is a name-brand open-frame around 3,500 running watts with a CO shutoff, like the Westinghouse WGen3600cv. It has enough power for a refrigerator, sump pump and lights, it shuts itself off when carbon monoxide builds up, and parts and a warranty stand behind it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap inverter generators any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Name-brand budget inverters like the Champion 201183, Westinghouse iGen2550c and WEN 56235i are good for what they are: quiet, light and clean enough for electronics. They cost more per running watt than open-frames, and around 1,800-1,900 running watts limits them to essential loads. No-name inverters are a gamble on parts and support.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many watts do I need from a cheap generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Add up the running watts of everything you want to run at once, then add the starting surge of the largest motor load. A refrigerator, sump pump, lights and a router typically land between 2,500 and 4,000 running watts with surge. That is open-frame territory for most budget buyers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a cheap generator safe to run in a garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. No generator is safe to run in a garage, even with the door open, and a CO shutoff does not change that. Run it outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors and windows, and keep a battery CO alarm inside.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do cheap generators damage electronics?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not usually, but budget open-frames often have higher harmonic distortion than inverters, and some electronics such as furnace boards, variable-speed appliances and some UPS units can react badly. Inverters like the Champion 201183 list under 3% THD. For sensitive gear, use an inverter, a power station or a UPS.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is dual fuel worth it on a cheap generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes if the generator will be stored more than it is used. Propane does not go stale, so a dual-fuel unit is far more likely to start after a year in the garage. You give up a little power on propane; the DuroMax XP5500HX drops from 4,500 to 4,275 running watts, for example.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a cheap generator power my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the sense most people mean. A budget generator can run essential circuits through a transfer switch or interlock, but it cannot run central air, an electric range and a water heater together. For that you need a much larger portable or a standby unit; see our whole-house generator guides.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

